.SH NAME
niac-config-interface-set - Set speed, duplex, and status for a device interface


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBniac config interface set    [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Set speed, duplex, and status for a device interface in a NIAC config.

.PP
If the named interface does not exist on the device, it is created. By default
the input file is updated in place; pass --output to write to a separate file.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--admin-status\fP=""
	Admin status: up or down

.PP
\fB--duplex\fP=""
	Interface duplex: full, half, or auto

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for set

.PP
\fB--oper-status\fP=""
	Operational status: up, down, or testing

.PP
\fB--output\fP=""
	Output config path; defaults to updating input in place

.PP
\fB--speed\fP=0
	Interface speed in Mbps


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--storage-keep\fP=500
	Run history records to keep, pruned oldest first on start (0 keeps every run)

.PP
\fB--storage-path\fP=""
	Path to NIAC run history database (default: ~/.niac/niac.db)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  niac config interface set network.yaml switch-a Ethernet1/1 --speed 1000 --duplex full
  niac config interface set network.yaml switch-a Ethernet1/1 --admin-status down --oper-status down
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBniac-config-interface(1)\fP


